WEBVTT

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.230
Second Players' Match, First Half
East Hand 1

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.230
Dealer: Nijou Izumi

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.190
<b>Shiraitodai High School</b>

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.020
<b>Free For All</b>

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.550
Welcome back, Teru!

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:50.860
Good job!

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.680
I got some sweets from the market.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.960
Um, I'll make some tea...

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.370
You got away from the press quickly.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.310
That's because we're still in 
the middle of the semifinals.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.560
You didn't use "that" this time.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.420
You know, that whizzing thing.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.870
The players today were really tough,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.950
so it was hard to find an 
opportunity to use it.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.270
I'll try to use it in the finals, though.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.560
That girl from Senriyama...

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:29.540
Huh?

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.070
Nothing.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.590
<i>I wonder if that girl from 
Senriyama is all right.</i>

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.950
<b>Achiga Girls'</b>

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.950
Kuro, we're starting.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.550
Yes!

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.330
What are you doing?

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.270
A ritual to revive the Dragon.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.120
Huh?

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.880
I checked the play records 
for the kids' mahjong club.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.820
Back in grade school,

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.170
Kuro would occasionally discard 
a dora, if she needed to.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.240
It'd take a while before 
she'd get doras again,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.640
and the length of these times varied.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.470
However, the number of hands played until

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.060
they returned was almost always the same.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.310
It looks like even games with 
three or two players are okay.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.330
So you mean...

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.940
...you're going to play 
a lot before the finals

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.620
to revive her dora?!

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.650
That's what I was thinking.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.820
I'm going to play Kuro and 
raise her basic mahjong skills

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.200
and wait for the Dragon's revival!

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.710
Harue...

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:33.880
<i>For the finals...</i>

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.860
Even if we can revive your dora, 
and make it to the finals,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.720
there's no guarantee that we can pull off

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.760
a cooperative play like before.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.730
Cooperative?

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.260
Kuro, didn't you notice?

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.500
I do think that Miyanaga Teru

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.900
had to declare riichi because 
you had all the dora,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.680
but that's not all.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.200
Shindouji's Hanada-san 
called a tile and

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.160
wrecked the champion's 
chance at an ippatsu tsumo.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.910
Then Senriyama's Onjouji-san 
made her take that last tile.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.720
<i>Hanada-san...</i>

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.000
<i>She had the biggest smile even though</i>

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.420
<i>she was the most discouraged.</i>

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.940
<i>Onjouji-san was so weak and still...</i>

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.900
They didn't do that for you.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.160
Why don't you look at the play 
records and the video later?

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.520
<i>You'll be seeing them 
many times from now on.</i>

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.500
There's nothing wrong with feeling down,

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.280
but right now, you have to move forward.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.600
Right.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.360
I stopped moving forward, once.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.290
Use that as an example of what <i>not</i> to do.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.730
Feel down if you want to,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.030
so long as you're moving 
ahead while you're at it.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:55.370
Yes!

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.300
East Hand 1
Seventh Turn

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:01.140
<i>Kuro-chan...</i>

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.760
Achiga Girls' (Nara)
 3rd Year - Second Player 
Matsumi Yuu

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.310
<i>Onjouji-senpai chose to close the gap</i>

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.010
<i>between us and Shiraitodai 
instead of securing second place.</i>

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.980
<i>Our coach said the same thing.</i>

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.440
Senriyama's goal is not to 
stand on the final stage.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.690
Our goal is to be on top!

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.060
<i>We'll take out Shiraitodai.</i>

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.270
<i>Twice, if we have to.</i>

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.720
Shiraitodai High School (West Tokyo)
3rd Year - Second Player 
Hirose Sumire

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.660
<i>But that's being idealistic.</i>

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.820
<i>Securing second here and</i>

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.230
<i>studying Shiraitodai in order to beat them</i>

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.000
<i>in the finals would be better.</i>

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:34.750
Senriyama Girls' (North Osaka) 
1st Year - Second Player 
Nijou Izumi

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:40.990
<i>In mahjong, that kind of thinking 
should work quite well.</i>

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.150
<i>Looks like Achiga might be in tenpai...</i>

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.430
<i>Safely, elegantly...</i>

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.480
<i>Time to close shop?</i>

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.390
Ron.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:51.720
3,900.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.100
Shindouji Girls' (Fukuoka)
3rd Year - Second Player
Yasukouchi Yoshiko

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.040
<i>She won without declaring 
riichi with </i>that<i> hand?</i>

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.110
Senriyama Girls' (North Osaka)
1st Year - Second Player
Nijou Izumi

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.100
<i>With </i>this<i> point gap?</i>

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.740
<i>What is she thinking?!</i>

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:04.790
Ron.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.450
2,600.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:09.490
Yes.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.330
<i>Again.</i>

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.540
<i>If you think about the points, 
shouldn't you go for a single suit?</i>

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.960
<i>Her style is totally different 
from the day before yesterday.</i>

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.800
<i>She wasn't the type of player who 
repeatedly won small hands, like this.</i>

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:26.300
<i>It's not like her wins are 
going up like with Teru,</i>

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.430
<i>but I'll still shoot her down.</i>

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.770
East Hand 3

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.960
Look at this! Shiraitodai's Hirose 
Sumire is in an ideal tenpai!

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:41.810
Wait, what?

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.330
What's this?

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.460
She went with the worse wait!

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:46.620
She's probably adjusting.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:48.390
Her cleavage?

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:48.220
Giddy

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:49.670
What?! No!

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.030
What are you talking about?

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.410
You mean, she'll adjust her wait 
for the eventual winning tile.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.080
You knew?!

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.830
That's what Shiraitodai's 
Hirose does best.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:04.480
She targets one player for a direct hit,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.510
like an archer taking aim.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.560
<i>Hirose Sumire's target is Shindouji.</i>

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:20.730
Ron.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:25.990
8,000.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:27.940
<i>It was me?!</i>

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.190
She shot her down in a flash!

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.650
She's Shiraitodai's sharpshooter!

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.500
Hirose Sumire!

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.290
<i>Hirose Sumire's great at getting a direct hit</i>

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.640
<i>on the player she chooses.</i>

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.500
<i>Wouldn't it be normal</i>

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.590
<i>to make last place go bust and take the win?</i>

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.080
<i>It's not because I look down on you.</i>

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.000
<i>It's because I'm wary.</i>

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.050
<i>To keep our chances in the finals high,</i>

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.470
<i>we've got to keep an 
eye on all three schools.</i>

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:58.560
<i>All the way to the fifth player.</i>

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.230
<i>We've fallen to third place.</i>

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.360
<i>If Onjouji-senpai hadn't helped out Achiga...</i>

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.380
<i>No. It might have been 
worse if Miyanaga Teru</i>

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.000
<i>had continued her winning streak.</i>

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.010
<i>No, I know that's not it!</i>

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.980
<i>It's my fault that we 
just got knocked down!</i>

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.870
East Hand 4

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.870
Sixth Turn

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.810
<i>Damn, I read that completely wrong.</i>

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.600
<i>She should be aiming at Achiga next.</i>

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.300
Tsumo.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.370
1,300. 2,600.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:30.510
<i>Again?</i>

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:32.510
<i>Shindouji, again!</i>

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:36.260
<i>It's not like Hirose Sumire 
can shoot down every hand,</i>

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:37.770
<i>but it's Shindouji again!</i>

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.760
<i>Shindouji's Yasukouchi-san is playing</i>

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.390
<i>completely differently from previous matches.</i>

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.520
<i>She goes for cheap hands 
instead of aiming for more.</i>

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.620
<i>Did she develop a new playing style,</i>

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.280
<i>now that she's experienced 
a second round match?</i>

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:51.820
<i>That's right.</i>

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.950
<i>She's a third year from 
Kyuushuu's veteran school.</i>

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.280
<i>She's not going to play 
in some predictable way.</i>

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.290
<i>Wait a second.</i>

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.600
<i>Everyone else here is a third year.</i>

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:03.650
<i>No, no.</i>

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.590
<i>In Senriyama's long history,</i>

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.120
<i>only a few first years have become regulars.</i>

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.030
<i>I am one of the very few.</i>

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:12.950
<i>In the inter-middle-school tournament,</i>

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.720
<i>individually, I didn't win as 
many points as Haramura Nodoka,</i>

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.640
<i>but I was able to do well 
in the group matches.</i>

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:21.480
<i>Right now, I should be the 
strongest first year in Japan.</i>

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:22.690
<i>Don't get freaked out now!</i>

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.290
<i>Let's do this.</i>

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.650
<i>All right.</i>

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:31.860
<i>One tile from tenpai.</i>

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:36.410
Ron.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:41.160
2,600.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.740
<i>She went for me again!</i>

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:51.960
Yes.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.210
<i>Yuu?</i>

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:59.720
Are you still on greenhouse duty?

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.580
The Inter-high is soon. Why don't you 
get someone to switch with you?

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.430
But it's so warm here.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.740
Plus, there are a lot of flowers 
here that my mom liked.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.370
I haven't been in the multimedia 
room in a long time.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.720
You don't use it for class?

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.200
Not lately.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.220
What are we here for?

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.040
Yes.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.350
From last year's champion school,

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.760
Shiraitodai High School: Hirose Sumire.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.750
She's a third year and their second player.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:37.590
If we are to make it to the finals,

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:39.450
you will be playing against her.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:41.570
I pulled some strings and got

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.880
loads of her play records and videos.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.420
I've watched them over and over.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.540
Watch her right hand.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:49.130
Yes!

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:55.770
Well?

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:56.660
Huh?

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:57.410
Didn't you see?

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.330
Her right hand moves back a couple 
of millimeters for a moment.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:04.540
I don't see it.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:06.550
Let me enlarge it a bit.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.250
Oh, I get it!

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.800
Right after this gesture, 
her gaze wanders a tiny bit.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.170
The one she looks at is her target.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.430
I couldn't tell just from her look,

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.170
but combined with the hand movement,

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:22.680
you can tell who'll be her next target.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.290
<i>So watch her hand's movement!</i>

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.430
South Hand 2

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.430
Dealer - Matsumi Yuu

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:28.900
<i>She moved.</i>

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:30.690
<i>Her target is...</i>

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:34.590
<i>...me.</i>

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.790
<i>She's set her arrow on me.</i>

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.450
<i>If I just continue 
straight down my path,</i>

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.950
<i>regardless of calling tiles 
or how my hand advances,</i>

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.170
<i>she'll shoot me down easily.</i>

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:56.420
<i>That's why</i>

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.130
<i>I have to change my movements 
and confuse my opponent.</i>

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:00.500
<i>I'll leave the path,</i>

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.100
<i>and change my destination.</i>

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:09.940
<i>She dodged...</i>

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.320
<i>Was that a coincidence or what?</i>

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.720
<i>Even if it's possible that 
you can coincidentally</i>

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.840
<i>dodge her attack by being too 
cautious or just plain bad,</i>

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.580
<i>Hirose Sumire will 
always set up a second shot.</i>

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:32.880
<i>However, by then, 
she will be forcing it.</i>

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.590
<i>When she's forcing 
herself is when you...</i>

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:39.050
Ron.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:40.810
12,000.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:42.840
<i>...have your opening.</i>

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:44.280
<i>What?</i>

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.560
<i>Not only did she avoid my attack, 
I played into her hand?!</i>

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.750
<i>I knew Achiga's Matsumi Yuu was used to</i>

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.730
<i>tricky plays because of her 
skewed tile distribution,</i>

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:57.670
<i>but I wouldn't have thought she</i>

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.490
<i>could surpass Hirose Sumire, 
even temporarily.</i>

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.990
<i>At this rate, I'll fall behind.</i>

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:03.660
<i>I've got no choice.</i>

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.330
<i>I'll have to try again.</i>

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.120
South Hand 2

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.120
Bonus 1

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:21.970
<i>She dodged it again!</i>

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:24.730
Tsumo.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:26.180
2,100 from all.

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:32.230
<i>No,</i>

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.480
<i>Trying again might be dangerous.</i>

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.390
<i>I'll have to sit back and 
watch Achiga for a while.</i>

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.490
Hey.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:42.350
Yeah?

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.130
Does Sumire have some kind of tell?

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:45.990
A tell?

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.600
Dunno. I never worried about that.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.460
Yeah, huh.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:52.000
Huh?

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.960
You mean her tell has been found out?

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:56.280
Probably.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:58.890
If we find out what it is...

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:01.090
We can tell her about it in the break!

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.930
<i>I hope that tell isn't 
something she needs, though.</i>

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:12.720
<i>Will we make it in time?</i>

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.060
Ron.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:17.460
3,200.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.440
<i>This was another wasted win.</i>

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.770
<i>Is she prioritizing speed that much?</i>

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.820
You're marvelous, Yasukouchi-senpai!

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:30.390
Yeah, but it was another 
cheap, undeveloped hand.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.320
It's probably what Yoshiko considers best,

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.340
under the circumstances.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:35.500
Captain...

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.410
Let's believe in Yoshiko.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.130
Yeah!

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.880
South Hand 3

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.880
Dealer - 
Yasukouchi Yoshiko

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.840
<i>Right now, my mission</i>

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.600
<i>is to avoid going bust,</i>

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.970
<i>and to gather some points 
for the next match.</i>

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:55.430
<i>I'm not weak either, 
when I play normally.</i>

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.210
Tsumo!

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:01.070
1,600. 3,200.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:02.730
<i>What's this?</i>

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.610
<i>I don't think I'll ever win a 
single hand from these people!</i>

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:09.620
I can't find Sumire's tell.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:11.330
Not at all!

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.580
We won't find it before the second half.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:16.300
She'll just have to do her usual best this time.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:18.240
<i>Sumire.</i>

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:25.510
<i>Why?</i>

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:28.070
<i>I'm moving towards winning 
as fast as possible,</i>

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.010
<i>I'm pushing forward as hard as I can,</i>

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.550
<i>but I can't even get into tenpai!</i>

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:34.310
<i>There's always someone better.</i>

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:35.860
Ron.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:36.810
1,300.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.080
Good game...

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:41.560
The second players' match...

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.520
...is over!

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:45.530
<i>This is bad.</i>

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:49.250
Good game.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.840
Good game.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.990
You just said that.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:55.740
What? Really?!

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:56.650
Yeah.

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.230
Sorry you had to follow my spacing out.

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:00.250
That's spacing out?

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:01.740
That was a good game.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:02.740
Good game.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.730
You didn't all have to follow me,

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.130
but thanks!

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:17.910
What? Can't go back?

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:20.520
Senpai!

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.560
I was like that too, last year.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:25.770
Didn't want to show my face.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.490
Yeah. I'm sorry.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.900
Hey. That's just how it goes.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.450
I don't get it.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.520
You've got next year and 
the year after that.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:38.870
Just get revenge and all's good.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.170
But you guys only have this year.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.330
And I...

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:44.670
Silly.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:47.420
That's why we're going 
to give our best.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.050
Senpai!

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:54.800
If you go like that, Funakubo-senpai 
will give you hell.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:55.940
Crap!

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.200
<b>Scold!</b>

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:57.660
I can't?

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:58.470
No.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:01.750
You go to the hospital.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:02.750
Yes!

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:06.940
And bring Ryuuka back here 
before the fifth match begins.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:09.690
I will.

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:17.850
And now...

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:24.420
Okay.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:25.790
Stay focused.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:29.120
It's all on you! Go, go, Ako!

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:29.880
Ako?

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:31.070
Huh?

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:33.650
Eguchi Sera is stronger now than 
she was in the second round match.

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:34.520
Right.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.020
I know that!

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.040
Yuu-nee!

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:47.150
Ako-chan!

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.450
You were super stable!

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:51.790
You've had a positive score since the regionals.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:53.650
Today, it was thanks to Akado-san.

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:55.710
I'm not going to lose, either.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:16:59.410
What?

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:04.010
I'm warming you up because you 
did your best... Just kidding.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.120
You're kidding?

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.450
You've been on a winning streak.

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:09.560
I thought maybe some of your 
luck would rub off on me.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:10.780
Some of your boobs, too.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:13.670
That's fine. If I can help...

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:16.430
Thanks.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.760
<i>I'm not shaking anymore.</i>

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.810
Thanks for that game the 
day before yesterday.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.980
Thank you for remembering.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.070
<b>Senriyama Girls'</b>

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:35.210
I'm back.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:36.320
Good job.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:38.280
We're alone?

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:40.700
I'm not happy about it.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.540
I'm really sorry about the match!

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:46.210
What?

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:47.170
Oh, yeah.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:48.680
They took quite a few points from you.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:51.890
But then, it was those opponents, 
and they were all third years.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:53.430
That sort of thing does happen.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:56.260
Age isn't an excuse, though.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.260
No, it does matter.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:03.560
These are the Nationals.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:06.750
Tsumo.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.010
2,000. 3,900.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.570
<i>It's Senriyama's Eguchi Sera!</i>

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:16.450
<i>She's winning too much!</i>

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:20.460
<i>In the second round match, 
I was a bit on the defensive,</i>

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.010
<i>but now I'm on the offensive.</i>

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.570
<i>Our team right now is 
second best in the nation.</i>

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.580
<i>I don't consider that our destination.</i>

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:32.130
<i>Right now, our ultimate goal 
is to beat Shiraitodai twice!</i>

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:35.760
South Hand 1

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:35.760
Dealer - Atarashi Ako

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:39.590
Ron.

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:41.350
7,700.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:44.060
Wow, you're good.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:48.510
<i>Last year, I was Senriyama's 
ace as a second year,</i>

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:51.140
<i>but that didn't do me any 
good in the Nationals.</i>

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.520
<i>The third years who were supposed 
to be weaker than me</i>

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:56.960
<i>suddenly showed an incredible 
power and played well</i>

00:18:56.960 --> 00:18:58.530
<i>once we got here to the Nationals.</i>

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:00.950
<i>I was really confused by that,</i>

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:03.240
<i>but now, I finally get it.</i>

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.120
Riichi.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:08.850
<i>Because I'm in that position now.</i>

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:11.350
Thank you!

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.320
According to my research data,

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:17.140
when third years play at a national level,

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.900
their average tenpai speed increases.

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:24.970
That's strange.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.340
Which is why it makes 
sense that a first year,

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:31.150
like yourself, would get beaten 
by those veteran third years.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.150
<i>I guess so.</i>

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:37.780
<i>It would happen.</i>

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:40.430
Tsumo.

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:41.470
4,000 from all.

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:47.750
<i>Does she have to keep 
winning as the dealer?!</i>

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:50.870
<i>Don't you realize Shibuya 
Takami is here?!</i>

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:53.330
<i>That's it.</i>

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:54.960
<i>I'll get things moving!</i>

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:01.610
But aren't there those strong, 
monster-like first years,

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.090
who can beat third years, as well?

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.100
Yeah, there are.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:07.970
Then I'm useless!

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.140
Last year, I was a young first year kid, too.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.030
I wasn't a regular,

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.200
but last year's first year representatives were

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:20.960
Arakawa Kei, Amae Koromo, and Jindai Komaki.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:24.920
They were in a totally different world,

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:27.990
so I never thought I'd be a match for them.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.880
I'm sure we'll have amazing first years

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.670
appear this year, as well.

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:37.800
Do you think you'll be one of them?

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:40.360
I was hoping so.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:43.070
As of now, you haven't 
produced sufficient results.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:48.510
Who do you think are this year's 
excellent first year players?

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:55.330
Hmm, I'd really watch out for 
Shiraitodai's Oohoshi Awai.

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:57.930
When she's at her best, I think 
even captain Shimizudani

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.610
would have problems.

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.790
Also, that first year from Kiyosumi High School.

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:04.730
You mean Haramura?

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:06.870
Miyanaga Saki.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.030
Rinshan kaihou may be her specialty,

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:12.110
but that's not all she's got.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:13.660
She's probably...

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.960
related to the champion, or something.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:18.920
But you know, Izumi,

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.210
don't get too caught up in comparing 
yourself with other people.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:25.550
I guess that's the wisdom
 of experience?

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:26.890
Oh, yeah.

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:30.500
I sent you Onjouji-senpai's hospital 
name and her room number.

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:31.560
Really?

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:33.040
Then I'll go see her.

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:34.560
Wait.

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:37.240
I want you to buy some bread from 
a convenience store on the way,

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:39.860
and force captain Shimizudani to eat some.

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.400
Got it.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:50.410
Will you be okay here by yourself?

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.000
Stupid. It's not like I'm scared.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:54.960
Got it.

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.710
I guess I should contact other club members.

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:02.730
<i>The first half is over!</i>

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:03.300
Huh?

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:08.370
The overwhelming point scorer was 
Senriyama Girls' Eguchi Sera!

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:12.010
Senriyama Girls' (North Osaka) 
3rd Year - Third Player 
Eguchi Sera

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:10.350
In just the first half, she added on

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.220
an amazing 36,000 points!

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:14.090
Continuing on, Achiga Girls'

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.960
Achiga Girls' (Nara)
 1st year - Third Player
 Atarashi Ako

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:15.350
has held on to second place,

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.100
and is back to their starting point total.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:21.130
Shiraitodai's Shibuya has been 
whittled down quite a bit,

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:22.980
Shiraitodai High School (West Tokyo)
 2nd Year - Third Player
 Shibuya Takami

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:23.230
but she seems to have
 an ace up her sleeve.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.690
Shindouji has fallen farther behind third place.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:31.660
Shindouji Girls' (Fukuoka)
3rd Year - Third Player 
Ezaki Hitomi

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:28.870
Will they be able to recover?

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.660
<i>Everything is because of bad politics!</i>

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:36.270
I'm not getting any doras.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:39.080
I'm getting so many, it's hilarious!

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:40.320
I'm back!

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:41.930
Good work.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:44.140
Did you see anything strange?

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.300
Not especially.

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:47.680
Shibuya was just what I expected.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.230
I knew it.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:51.580
Knew what?

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:53.780
Shiraitodai's Shibuya Takami.

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:54.890
The girl with glasses.

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:56.680
The slightly round faced girl?

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:58.440
Oh yeah, that girl.

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:00.440
What about her?

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:04.570
That girl has a history of getting 
a yakuman in the last hand.

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:05.790
Wow!

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:10.450
Generally, one would think that's all 
there was to it, but there's a pattern.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.130
All the initial tile discards by Shibuya Takami

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:16.560
in the hands prior to the last would then

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:18.500
come to her starting hand in the last hand.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.740
That means, if an East-South 
match has a lot of draws

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:24.430
or dealer winning streaks, with some luck,

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:26.230
she could even get a Tenhou hand.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:27.460
(Tenhou: yakuman hand where the dealer has a winning hand from the beginning)

00:23:26.230 --> 00:23:27.460
On the other hand...

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:31.340
If an East-South match goes as quickly 
as possible, with only eight hands,

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:33.510
she'll only be able to collect 
seven tiles at most.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.530
That's still scary.

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:37.270
She can easily make a 
daisangen or something like that.

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:37.270
(Daisangen: yakuman hand with 3 of
 the red, white, & green dragons)

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.070
Shibuya Takami only started 
playing as a regular this year,

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:43.710
so we couldn't be sure since there 
weren't many play records for her,

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:45.270
but Harue noticed this pattern.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.100
So, to counter her, we need to 
avoid dealer winning streaks,

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:50.450
and to make sure the dealer is 
not in tenpai when it's a draw.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:51.720
That's it!

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:53.620
A pretty easy strategy, I'd say.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.620
<i>Or so I thought!</i>

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:57.620
<i>This is bad!</i>

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.330
Third Players' Match, Second Half

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.330
East Hand 2

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:01.620
<i>I'm the dealer and got a tsumo!</i>

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:03.920
<i>Now what do I do with this?</i>

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:06.950
<i>I guess, if Shibuya Takami 
gets to set eight tiles</i>

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:09.860
<i>instead of seven, 
that would still be okay...</i>

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:13.430
<i>Would Nodoka be able to calculate 
what would be better here?</i>

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:16.600
<i>I just don't know!</i>

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:18.390
Tsumo.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:20.440
How much?

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:22.400
1,000 from all.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.040
<i>I blew it...</i>

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.410
<i>If it had been more 
expensive at least...</i>

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:28.070
Bonus 1.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:29.650
East Hand 2

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:29.650
Bonus 1

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:34.630
<i>Tenpai. 11,600, even like this.</i>

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.740
<i>But I can't really be 
happy about this...</i>

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:43.030
Ro—

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:48.310
Ron... 11,900.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:49.500
Okay.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:52.260
<i>It was a lot, so it's okay, right?</i>

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.260
East Hand 2

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.260
Bonus 2

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:56.990
<i>What should I do if I get 
a hand together, again?</i>

00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:58.160
Riichi!

00:24:58.950 --> 00:24:59.520
Pon!

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:03.770
<i>Nice trick with that pon.</i>

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:07.260
Tsumo! 2,200. 4,200.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.480
<i>Oh, well.</i>

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:12.570
East Hand 3

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:12.570
Dealer: Shibuya Takami

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:14.570
Ron.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:16.380
<i>That was fast!</i>

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:18.120
<i>Okay.</i>

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:20.030
<i>She's going on a streak herself.</i>

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.660
<i>Now she has ten tiles for sure.</i>

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:24.500
East Hand 3

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:24.500
Bonus 1

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:25.900
Tsumo!

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:28.500
2,100. 4,100.

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:31.420
<i>Geez, she keeps winning 
these expensive hands,</i>

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:33.010
<i>that tomboy from Senriyama!</i>

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:36.180
<i>Isn't that going to turn into a 
weakness of hers or something?</i>

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:36.180
Thank you.

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:39.090
Chi!

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:43.970
She goes for it!

00:25:43.970 --> 00:25:46.910
Atarashi plays like this a lot.

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:47.400
Huh?

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:49.970
If you don't have any honor tiles 
or aren't near a tanyao,

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:51.650
and are aiming to win fast,

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:54.210
the question is if it's feasible by calling

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:56.070
to get to a sanshoku or ittsu.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.650
That can be used to get ahead a bit,

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.330
and whether it's done well or poorly,

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:03.660
I feel like this is Atarashi's forte.

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:05.960
Oh, I see.

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.860
Yes... Wait, we're working here!

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.950
Oops, Eguchi is in tenpai!

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:11.590
Riichi.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:14.780
Ron, 3900.

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:18.070
<i>So that didn't go through.</i>

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:20.070
<i>She's meticulous, as always.</i>

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:22.810
South Hand 1

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:25.040
<i>Until now, Shibuya Takami's 
initial discards were</i>

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:30.380
<i>red dragon, white dragon, south, green dragon, 
north, white dragon, red dragon, red dragon.</i>

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:31.570
<i>So a daisangen.</i>

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:34.070
I'm dealer, so riichi!

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:36.940
<i>Dealer riichi?</i>

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.460
<i>If Shindouji goes on a streak,</i>

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:42.830
<i>Shibuya Takami will have 11 
tiles for the last round.</i>

00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:44.500
<i>I can't do much with this hand,</i>

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:46.870
<i>so I'll have to hope for 
Eguchi Sera to come through.</i>

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:48.370
Tenpai.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:49.690
No-ten.

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:50.790
Draw

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:50.790
<i>Useless.</i>

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:53.800
<i>Damn it, now I'm just going to 
force the game to keep moving!</i>

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:57.280
Ron. 1,300.

00:26:57.820 --> 00:26:59.640
<i>Nice, I got the riichi wager!</i>

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:01.890
<i>I'm dealer...</i>

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:04.540
<i>Should I just close shop,</i>

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:06.280
<i>or give up the last hand and try to win</i>

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:07.800
<i>as many points as possible?</i>

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.770
<i>All right, I'll go for a win!</i>

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.770
South Hand 2

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.770
7th Turn

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:15.400
Tsumo. 3,000. 6,000.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.930
<i>Another big one?!</i>

00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:20.820
<i>We were overtaken!</i>

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:23.080
Senriyama has taken second place!

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.530
South Hand 3

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.530
Dealer: Shibuya Takami

00:27:26.260 --> 00:27:26.830
Pon.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:29.830
<i>She's moving fast for a streak.</i>

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:31.830
<i>I won't let you!</i>

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:34.230
Chi!

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:38.300
<i>I won't lose at high speeds!</i>

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:40.220
Riichi.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.180
Ron. 3,900.

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:50.190
<i>Oh, she stopped my riichi 
with 39 again. Fun.</i>

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:53.270
<i>And now... it's time.</i>

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:00.500
<i>The last round.</i>

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.350
<i>The seeds I've sown will turn 
into plants and bear fruit...</i>

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:20.770
<i>Harvest time!</i>

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:55.550
<b>Aspiration</b>

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:43.930
Hey, what kind of fish do you 
think are in Tokyo's rivers?

00:29:43.930 --> 00:29:45.480
A long time ago, I saw on the news

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:46.720
that there are seals in them.

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:47.650
Really?!

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:48.950
We have to book a boat!

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:50.720
What kind of bait 
should we take for them?!

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.810
Wait... You're going to fish them?
